<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of al-Abwab</h1><p class="st-meta">1276 CE – 1400 CE (approx.) · capital Kabushiya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Christian Nubian kingdom on the Nile south of Makuria, once a province of Alodia, known mainly from Mamluk-era Arabic sources.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Al-Abwab, Arabic for 'the gates', was originally the northern province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/alodia">Alodia</a>, centred near Kabushiya close to the junction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atbara-river">Atbara</a>. By 1276 it appears as an independent kingdom, apparently having broken away as Alodian power declined.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Almost nothing is known of its internal government. In 1276 its king Adur captured the fugitive Makurian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, who had fled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate's</a> invasion, and handed him over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a>. Adur later corresponded with Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalawun">Qalawun</a>, offering alliance against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/makuria">Makuria</a>. Its people were Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The kingdom fades from the record in the late 14th century, probably absorbed by advancing Arab tribes and the general collapse of Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>. The area later fell within the sphere of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/abdallab-sheikhdom">Abdallab Sheikhdom</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-al-abwab.jpg" alt="Kingdom of al-Abwab" width="320" height="282"><figcaption>Image: LeGabrie · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Abwab.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1276 CE – 1400 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kabushiya</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Adur</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity (Nubian Church)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Nubian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sudan">Sudan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>al-Abwab</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1338&amp;focus=kingdom-of-al-abwab" aria-label="Kingdom of al-Abwab on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5473 1574 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of al-Abwab lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5892 1856h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5793 1830h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5793 1830h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Sudan. <a href="/atlas?year=1338&amp;focus=kingdom-of-al-abwab">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
